# FourForces Watch: project rules

## Architecture (keep the layers separate)
- **L0 adapters** (`adapters/<force>/<source>.py`): one module per public source per force.
- **L1 force rules** (`forces/`): turn observations into an ok / watch / alert status per neighborhood.
- **L2 city packs** (`cities/<city>.yaml`): all city-specific values (bbox, stations, zones, neighborhoods, thresholds).
  Never hard-code a city in an adapter. Read it from the `city` dict.
- **L3 open API + dashboard** (`app.py`, `web/`): JSON, GeoJSON (`/api/geojson`), CAP 1.2 (`/api/cap`), status (`/api/status`).

## Adapter interface
Every adapter module exposes:
```python
NAME = "source_name"          # unique, snake_case
FORCE = "water" | "air" | "land" | "fire"
def fetch(city: dict) -> list[Observation]: ...
```
- Return only `core.schema.Observation` objects:
  - fields: force, source, place{id,name,lat,lon}, time (UTC ISO), measurement, value, unit, threshold, status, extra;
  - `status` is one of ok / watch / alert / unknown.
- Missing key or config → `raise SkipAdapter("reason")`. Never crash the refresh loop.
- Use `core.http.client()` (polite User-Agent and timeouts). Respect each feed's terms. Never store third-party camera imagery.
- Add the feed to the README feed table and to `cities/_template.yaml` if it needs config.

## Secrets
- Never commit secrets, keys, tokens or `.env`. Read them from environment variables only (`XAI_API_KEY`,
  `AIRNOW_API_KEY`, `PURPLEAIR_API_KEY`, `FIRMS_MAP_KEY`) and document new ones in `.env.example` with an empty value.
- Code must degrade gracefully without any key: cameras show "not checked", messages fall back to templates.

## Tests
- Every new adapter, rule or export needs a test in `tests/`. Tests must run offline: parse recorded sample payloads
  or monkeypatch HTTP, never hit live APIs.
- Run `pytest -q` before merging. CI (`.github/workflows/tests.yml`) runs it on every push and pull request.

## Workflow
- Work on a feature branch (`feature/water`, `feature/air`, `feature/land`, `feature/fire`, `feature/dashboard`,
  `feature/demo`, `docs/roadmap`) → tests pass → merge to `main` → deploy. Don't rewrite published history.

## Open-source hygiene
- MIT license. Public, generic civic-tech content only: no proprietary, company or client content, no private
  roadmaps, no vendor recommendations.
- Simulated / demo data must always be labelled SIMULATED in the UI and API (`extra.simulated = true`).
- This is not an official warning system. Wording points users to NWS and county emergency management.
